\section*{I. INTRODUCTION}
Hybrid surface waves are surface waves propagating at the interface between surface plasmon polaritons (SPPs) and phonon-polaritons. They have been observed in two-dimensional metamaterials \cite{li2011hybrid, li2011hybrid2} and demonstrated in three-dimensional metamaterials \cite{li2012hybrid} for applications in nanophotonics and plasmonics. These hybrid surface waves are surface waves exhibiting distinctive characteristics and superior performance. Specifically, hybrid surface waves possess wavelengths exceeding the diffraction limit of light \cite{boehmer2011surface}, offering advantages over localized plasmons. Additionally, they enable the manipulation of surface waves through plasmonic metamaterials \cite{li2011hybrid}, providing advantages over photonic crystals. Hydrodynamic hybrid surface waves hold the potential for superior attributes, yet they remain theoretically unexplored. This paper proposes an alternative approach to achieving and demonstrating hybrid surface wave propagation at the interface between metal-dielectric rods of subwavelength dimensions within one-dimensional metamaterials. These hybrid surface waves rely on the collective resonant properties of metal-dielectric rods under illumination. By selecting an appropriate plasmonic metamaterial, the resulting hybrid surface waves can exhibit wavelengths 1.5 times greater than the light wavelength.

